WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.834
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-Nominated actor

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:05.067
who you know
from "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:08.000
She is making her New York
stage debut in the play,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.200
"The Search for Signs

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.000
of Intelligent Life
in the Universe,"

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.534
which is running now through
February 6th at The Shed.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:17.300
Everyone, please welcome
Cecily Strong!

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.100
-Hi, bud!
-Hi.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.367
Hi, Jimmy.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.467
-Cecily, welcome back
to the show.

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:26.968
It's so great to see you.

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:28.601
-Oh, thank you.

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:31.634
Love being back virtual.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:33.400
-I know, but I guess

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.400
you have to be extra,
extra, extra careful.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.367
-Yes, I'm living a very
monastic lifestyle right now.

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:43.901
-Yeah.
-A fantastic monastic lifestyle.

00:00:43.901 --> 00:00:45.100
-Yeah.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.467
I loved you on "Saturday Night
Live," obviously,

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:48.834
and I've been lucky enough
to work with you, too,

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:54.234
but this is kind of
an insane thing.

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:56.267
You get to play --
-Oh.

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.033
-How many characters
do you play in this?

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:02.667
-I think I play 11
at my last count, but I'm --

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.868
There's other characters
that I'm talking to

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:06.901
that I'm not playing,
but, yes, it's --

00:01:06.901 --> 00:01:09.000
-Wow.
-It's pretty nuts.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.267
I feel like I've gone
a little bit nuts.

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:16.167
I think even somehow in a mask,
I can still --

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:20.133
people still know I'm talking
to myself, walking around.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:23.501
-Just practicing your lines?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:25.434
-How does --
-And I was surprised

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:27.767
that I was getting looks
even with the mask.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.167
I guess you can --
-How does it compare to "SNL"?

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:38.968
-Oh, it's -- you know,
it's very different.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:42.534
Just, number one, we don't
have cue cards, it turns out.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.634
-Oh, wow. They don't do that?
-They make you memorize...

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:46.400
-What?
-...other places.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.667
-They don't have cue cards?

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.968
-I thought that was in my
contract, but...

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:53.234
-I heard that it was
Jeanine Pirro --

00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:55.100
When you played Jeanine Pirro

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.300
very funny
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.834
that kind of got you this role.

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.334
Is that true?

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:01.267
-Yeah, she insisted.

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:04.968
Wouldn't that be funny
if Jeanine actually did?

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:10.200
No, our director actually saw
the finale from last season,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.534
and I guess, you know,
she saw me walking --

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:16.767
getting into a box of wine,
and she went like,

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:20.033
"That person's crazy enough
to take this on."

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:24.501
-[ Laughs ] The original --
The original show

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:29.701
starred Lily Tomlin and
Jane Wagner, who's the writer.

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.133
-Yes.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.334
-They're producing this?

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.534
They're executive producers
on this?

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:36.968
-Yes, they are,
and they're in L.A.,

00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:40.000
so I've only gotten to
really talk with them

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.734
on the phone and Zoom with them,

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:46.834
and we did a "New York Times"
article, and after --

00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:50.467
I think we got about an hour
with them and David afterwards,

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.934
Leigh -- my director --
and I turned to each other

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:55.567
and sobbed just 'cause
it was so --

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:57.934
it's just amazing
that they're trusting us

00:02:57.934 --> 00:03:00.334
and they're just
so funny in person.

00:03:00.334 --> 00:03:04.667
And then this is very telling --
Jane's story.

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:06.567
But they were -- you know,
they were like,

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:08.400
"Yeah, call us on opening."

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.834
So right after the show,

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.100
Lee and I
have the phone in our hand,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.634
and we call, and Jane said "Hi,"
and we went, "Hi!"

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:17.267
And then she went,
"Who is this?"

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.868
-That's a good bit.
-Yeah.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.167
If it was a bit.
We'll never know.

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:26.133
-Is there any way
to set this up?

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:30.701
-It's a --
We're redoing Lily's show,

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:32.400
but really, it's kind of --

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.801
it's exploring human connection,
basically, you know,

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:39.701
at a time when we haven't
really gotten to have it

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:42.467
and especially after theaters
have really been

00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:44.067
shut down for two years.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:47.667
It's also part of the show.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:51.601
It celebrates being in the
theater together in this moment,

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:54.100
and it's been so special
every night.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.901
It's such a surprising show
and, you know, some people

00:03:57.901 --> 00:04:00.934
are laughing at the same time
some people are crying,

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:03.968
and it's a very surreal,
lovely experience.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:05.534
-I was going to ask you --
-And I have, you know,

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.367
an umbrella hat on my head.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:10.634
-Yeah.
I mean is it tough for you to --

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:15.334
You studied theater, I mean,
before you got into comedy.

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:17.534
-Yes, I'm a -- I studied it.

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:21.133
I'm a very serious thespian.

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:23.601
You know,
I think I missed a step.

00:04:23.601 --> 00:04:28.100
I should have done, like,
a regular ensemble play.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.300
I went -- I jumped back in

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.000
at a one-woman show
playing 11 characters.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.667
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
That's the way to do it, see?

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:38.667
Don't do anything easy, buddy.
Just go in and just crush it.

00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:41.834
-No, no, no, yeah, you've got
to show people you're nuts.

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:45.033
-Yeah.
-Or you are, not your nuts.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:46.767
I guess you could --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.501
-Yeah, don't show -- Thank you.

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:50.200
-Don't show people your nuts...

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.033
-No.
-...unless they've invited you.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:54.968
-Don't show -- Yeah.
You are -- Yeah.

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:56.267
Can you believe, Cecily,

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:59.033
that you've been doing "SNL"
for 10 seasons?

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:03.300
-No, I mean,
it feels like 30 years sometimes

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.601
and then --
but it's always surprising.

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:11.734
I mean, the past two years,
it's been so up in the air.

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:15.400
So I'm still -- it still feels
new and fresh and --

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.501
-You're crushing it.

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:17.701
-I'm still learning,
and the only thing

00:05:17.701 --> 00:05:20.501
I think I have down
is cue cards, sort of.

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:22.234
-Yeah, I know.
You're really --

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:24.634
You actually are very good
at cue cards.

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:27.834
-Yeah, I mean I should be
using cue cards in the show,

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:31.801
but I don't know, artistic
decision of the director.

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:34.267
-I heard that your mom and dad

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:37.067
were at your "SNL" audition,
is that true?

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:40.133
-Yes, my dad and my stepmom
were there

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:41.767
at iO, at the showcase,

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.834
and they were sitting
in front of Lorne,

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:45.501
but they were --
they knew he was there

00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:47.901
and so they were so nervous.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.801
I saw them after, and they
looked -- they were so pale.

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:53.000
It's like they'd seen a ghost,
and it was like,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.567
"Wow, was I -- was it that bad?

00:05:56.567 --> 00:05:57.968
You could have --
you could have laughed."

00:05:57.968 --> 00:06:00.367
But then I realized
it was better that --

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:05.267
there was, like, another girl
there who was clearly, you know,

00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:09.434
trying to help out her friend
that was auditioning.

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:12.267
It was, like, she was in some
polka dot dress or something,

00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:15.200
like [Laughs]
really laughing so hard.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.667
-Laughing at every other bit,
yeah.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:19.968
-It was like --
Yeah, one of those, you know,

00:06:19.968 --> 00:06:22.734
like those theater shows
where the audience --

00:06:22.734 --> 00:06:25.367
the audience is in the play,
whatever it is.

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:28.100
-Yeah. When did you realize
that you were --

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.100
that comedy was your thing?

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:36.567
-You know, I was always making
my family laugh, at least.

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:38.167
You know, one of my first --

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:40.801
I had two really great
impressions since a little kid,

00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:44.901
and one was the shrunken head
man from "Beetlejuice,"

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.968
and then I also liked the alien

00:06:46.968 --> 00:06:50.534
that popped out of the guy's
stomach in "Spaceballs."

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:52.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.133 --> 00:06:53.300
-Yeah.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.234
-I should have done them
for my "SNL" audition.

00:06:55.234 --> 00:06:56.567
-Yeah, you really should have
done that one.

00:06:56.567 --> 00:06:57.801
Oh, my gosh, yeah.

00:06:57.801 --> 00:06:59.534
I think things are going
pretty well

00:06:59.534 --> 00:07:01.133
the way they are right now,
buddy.

00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:03.300
Cecily, I love
having you on the show.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.133
I can't wait to see you
on stage,

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:08.133
but also in person
here back at the show.

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:09.501
Congrats on all of this.
I appreciate you coming on.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:12.400
-Soon enough. Thanks, Jimmy.
-Cecily Strong, everybody.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.000
-Thank you.
-See her in "The Search

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.601
for Signs of Intelligent Life
in the Universe,"

00:07:16.601 --> 00:07:19.133
running now through February 6th
at The Shed.

